/-
# Problem Description

Throughout, `(X, O_X)` denotes a ringed space, i.e. a topological space `X`
together with a sheaf of (commutative) rings `O_X`. By an `O_X`-module we mean a
sheaf of `O_X`-modules. For an open `U ⊆ X` we write `O_U = O_X|_U`, and for a
finite collection of sections `s_1, ..., s_n ∈ F(U)` the *associated map* is the
`O_U`-module homomorphism

  `⊕_{i=1,...,n} O_U → F|_U,  (a_1,...,a_n) ↦ Σ_i a_i s_i,`

which on the `i`-th summand sends `1` to `s_i|_U`.

* Definition 1 (Generated by sections). `F` is generated by `s_1, ..., s_n ∈ F(X)`
  if the associated map `⊕ O_X → F` is surjective; more generally `F|_U` is
  generated by sections of `F(U)` if the associated map `⊕ O_U → F|_U` is
  surjective (equivalently, surjective on every stalk).

* Definition 2 (Finite type, Stacks Tag 01B5). `F` is of finite type if for every
  point `x ∈ X` there is an open neighbourhood `U ∋ x` such that `F|_U` is
  generated by finitely many sections.

* Definition 3 (Coherent, Stacks Tag 01BV). `F` is coherent if it is of finite
  type and, for every open `U ⊆ X` and every finite collection of sections
  `s_1, ..., s_n ∈ F(U)`, the kernel of the associated map `⊕ O_U → F|_U` is an
  `O_U`-module of finite type.

* Definition 4 (Finite presentation, Stacks Tag 01BN). `F` is of finite
  presentation if every point has an open neighbourhood `U` over which `F|_U` is
  the cokernel of a map `⊕_{j=1,...,m} O_U → ⊕_{i=1,...,n} O_U` between finite
  free modules.

* Definition 5 (Quasi-coherent, Stacks Tag 01BE). `F` is quasi-coherent if every
  point has an open neighbourhood `U` over which `F|_U` is the cokernel of a map
  `⊕_{j ∈ J} O_U → ⊕_{i ∈ I} O_U` between (possibly infinite) free modules.

# Main Statement (Stacks Tag 01BW)

If `F` is coherent then `F` is of finite presentation, and hence quasi-coherent.
Concretely:
  (1) coherent ⟹ finite presentation;
  (2) finite presentation ⟹ quasi-coherent.
-/

/-
## Formalization notes

We work over the ringed space `(X, O_X)`, modelling `O_X` as a sheaf of rings on
the site of opens of `X` (`Opens.grothendieckTopology X`), and `O_X`-modules as
terms of `SheafOfModules O_X`. This is precisely Mathlib's framework for sheaves
of modules over a ringed space.

We use the following existing Mathlib notions, all developed in exactly the
Stacks-project idiom (see the references in `Mathlib/.../Sheaf/Generators.lean`
and `Mathlib/.../Sheaf/Quasicoherent.lean`):

* `SheafOfModules.GeneratingSections M`     -- a family of sections generating `M`
                                               (the associated map `free I ⟶ M` is
                                               epi), i.e. Definition 1.
* `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`             -- Definition 2 (Stacks Tag 01B5).
* `SheafOfModules.IsFinitePresentation`     -- Definition 4 (Stacks Tag 01BN).
* `SheafOfModules.IsQuasicoherent`          -- Definition 5 (Stacks Tag 01BE).

Here `M.over U : SheafOfModules (O_X.over U)` is the restriction `F|_U`, and for
an index type `I`, `free I` is the free module `⊕_{i ∈ I} O_U`. The "associated
map" attached to a family of sections `s : I → (M.over U).sections` is
`(M.over U).freeHomEquiv.symm s : free I ⟶ M.over U`; by `freeHomEquiv` every map
out of a free module arises this way, so quantifying over finite families of
sections is the same as quantifying over maps `free I ⟶ M.over U` with `I` finite.

We only have to *add* the notion of coherence (Definition 3); the two
implications of the theorem are the content of the main statement.

The space, rings, module sections, and free-module index types live in an
arbitrary common universe, as supported by Mathlib's sheafification and
finite-presentation API. Commutativity of `O_X` is not needed for the statement,
so we allow `O_X` to be a sheaf of (not necessarily commutative) rings.
-/

open CategoryTheory SheafOfModules Limits TopologicalSpace

universe u w v₁ v₂ u₁ u₂

variable {X : Type u} [TopologicalSpace X]
  {R : Sheaf (Opens.grothendieckTopology X) RingCat.{u}}

section GeneralSite

/-
The four helper declarations below are stated for an arbitrary site.  Nothing in their proofs
uses the topology of `X`; a ringed space enters only through the `IsCoherent` class and the
main statements, which stay over `Opens X`.
-/
variable {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C} {Rs : Sheaf J RingCat.{u}}
  [∀ U : C, HasSheafify (J.over U) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [∀ U : C, (J.over U).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [∀ (U : C) (V : Over U), HasSheafify ((J.over U).over V) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [∀ (U : C) (V : Over U), ((J.over U).over V).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]

/-
## Helper declarations for implication (1) (coherent ⟹ finite presentation)

These auxiliary declarations do not depend on the `IsCoherent` class; they are
the reusable building blocks of `isFinitePresentation_of_isCoherent` and are
placed before the main definition so that the protected `IsCoherent`/theorem
pair stay adjacent.

In Mathlib's `SheafOfModules` framework, `M.IsFinitePresentation` unfolds to
`∃ q : M.QuasicoherentData, q.IsFinitePresentation`, where a `QuasicoherentData`
is a cover `X : I → Opens X` (`coversTop`) together with a `Presentation`
`(M.over (X i)).Presentation` for each `i`, and `IsFinitePresentation` requires
each such presentation to be `IsFinite` (finitely many generators and finitely
many relations).

The gluing of *local quasicoherent data over a cover* into *global quasicoherent
data* is exactly `SheafOfModules.QuasicoherentData.bind`: given a cover
`Xc : I → Opens X` (`hX : CoversTop Xc`) and, for each `i`, a
`QuasicoherentData (M.over (Xc i))`, it produces a `QuasicoherentData M`.
Crucially, `bind` *internally performs the slice-to-base transport* (via
`Over.iteratedSliceEquiv` and `pushforwardPushforwardEquivalence`) that the naive
hand-rolled proof would have to develop separately — its `i`-indexed presentation
is `((D i.1).presentation i.2).map _ _ |>.ofIsIso`, transported from the slice
`(M.over (Xc i)).over ((D i.1).X i.2)` down to the base open `((D i.1).X i.2).left`.

Hence the proof splits into two reusable lemmas:

* `bind_isFinitePresentation` (Lemma A): `bind` preserves finite presentation
  (if every per-open datum is finitely presented, so is their bind). This is the
  keystone that absorbs the slice transport.
* `over_quasicoherentData_isFP_of_finiteGenerators_finiteKernel` (Lemma B):
  over a single open `U`, finite generating sections of `M.over U` whose kernel
  is of finite type give a finitely-presented `QuasicoherentData (M.over U)`.
-/

/- **Lemma A (keystone): `bind` preserves finite presentation.**
If a cover `Xc` covers the top and, over each `Xc i`, the quasicoherent datum
`D i` is finitely presented, then the bound quasicoherent datum
`QuasicoherentData.bind M Xc hX D` is finitely presented.

This is the step that absorbs the "slice-to-base transport" obstruction: `bind`'s
`⟨i, j⟩`-indexed presentation is `Presentation.ofIsIso _ ((D i).presentation j).map _ _)`,
i.e. `(D i).presentation j` pushed along the equivalence
`pushforwardPushforwardEquivalence ((D i).X j).iteratedSliceEquiv …` and then
transported across an iso. Both operations preserve `Presentation.IsFinite`:

* `Presentation.ofIsIso f σ` is `IsFinite` whenever `σ` is (there is an instance
  `instIsFinite…OfIsIso`, found by `infer_instance`);
* `Presentation.map F η` preserves `IsFinite` because
  `Presentation.map_generators_I` and `Presentation.map_relations_I` give
  `(σ.map F η).generators.I = σ.generators.I` and likewise for relations, so the
  finiteness of the index types is transported by `rw`; the underlying functor
  `F` here is an equivalence and so `PreservesColimitsOfSize`.

The source finiteness `((D i).presentation j).IsFinite` is
`(hD i).isFinite_presentation j`.

PROOF SKETCH (one level): `refine ⟨fun ij => ?_⟩; obtain ⟨i, j⟩ := ij`; reduce
`(bind M Xc hX D).presentation ⟨i,j⟩` to its `ofIsIso (… .map F η)` normal form
(by `show`/`simp only` on the `bind` definitional unfolding, or by a helper
`bind_presentation` equation); establish `((D i).presentation j).map F η).IsFinite`
from `(hD i).isFinite_presentation j` via the two `map_*_I` rewrites packaged as a
small `have`; then `infer_instance` closes the `ofIsIso` wrapper. Watch out: this
is instance-synthesis-heavy at the `bind` term; keep the high `maxHeartbeats` /
`synthInstance.maxHeartbeats` set at the top of the file. A clean route is to first
prove a tiny auxiliary lemma `Presentation.IsFinite.map` (map preserves `IsFinite`)
and reuse the existing `ofIsIso` instance. -/

/-- `Presentation.map` preserves `IsFinite`: the finiteness of the generator and
relation index types is transported across `map_generators_I` / `map_relations_I`. -/
@[ega "0 5.2.5" "special case"]
instance Presentation.IsFinite.map {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
    {R : Sheaf J RingCat.{u}}
    [HasSheafify J AddCommGrpCat.{u}] [J.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
    [J.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ RingCat.{u} AddCommGrpCat.{u})]
    {C' : Type u₂} [Category.{v₂} C'] {J' : GrothendieckTopology C'} {S : Sheaf J' RingCat.{u}}
    [HasSheafify J' AddCommGrpCat.{u}] [J'.WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
    [J'.HasSheafCompose (forget₂ RingCat.{u} AddCommGrpCat.{u})]
    {M : SheafOfModules.{u} R} (P : M.Presentation)
    (F : SheafOfModules.{u} R ⥤ SheafOfModules.{u} S)
    [PreservesColimitsOfSize.{u, u} F]
    (η : unit S ≅ F.obj (unit R)) [P.IsFinite] :
    (P.map F η).IsFinite where
  isFiniteType_generators := by
    refine ⟨?_⟩
    rw [Presentation.map_generators_I]
    exact (Presentation.IsFinite.isFiniteType_generators (p := P)).finite
  isFiniteType_relations := by
    refine ⟨?_⟩
    rw [Presentation.map_relations_I]
    exact (Presentation.IsFinite.isFiniteType_relations (p := P)).finite

/-- If `Xc` covers the site and each `D i` is quasicoherent data for `M.over (Xc i)` all of
whose presentations are finite, then the glued quasicoherent data
`QuasicoherentData.bind M Xc hX D` on `M` consists of finite presentations. -/
theorem bind_isFinitePresentation
    (M : SheafOfModules.{u} Rs) {I : Type u}
    (Xc : I → C) (hX : J.CoversTop Xc)
    (D : (i : I) → (M.over (Xc i)).QuasicoherentData)
    [hD : ∀ i, (D i).IsFinitePresentation] :
    (QuasicoherentData.bind M Xc hX D).IsFinitePresentation := by
  constructor
  rintro ⟨i, j⟩
  have : ((D i).presentation j).IsFinite := (hD i).isFinite_presentation j
  delta QuasicoherentData.bind
  dsimp only
  infer_instance

/-- Per-slice presentation: over a slice open `V : Over U`, finite generating
sections `g` of `M.over U` (with associated map `g.π`) together with a generating
sections `r` of the restricted kernel `(kernel g.π).over V` assemble into a
`Presentation` of `(M.over U).over V`.

The generators are obtained by pushing `g.π` along the restriction functor
`F := pushforward (𝟙 _) = over V` (which preserves colimits, hence epis and free
objects): `e.hom ≫ F.map g.π : free g.I ⟶ (M.over U).over V` is an epi. The kernel
of this map is identified with `(kernel g.π).over V = F.obj (kernel g.π)` via
`PreservesKernel.iso` (`F` preserves kernels) and `kernelIsIsoComp` (kernel is
invariant under precomposition with the iso `e.hom`); transporting `r` along that
iso (an epi) gives the relations. -/
noncomputable def overPresentation (M : SheafOfModules.{u} Rs) (U : C) (V : Over U)
    (g : (M.over U).GeneratingSections) [Finite g.I]
    (r : ((kernel g.π).over V).GeneratingSections) :
    ((M.over U).over V).Presentation :=
  letI : Fintype g.I := Fintype.ofFinite g.I
  letI : DecidableEq g.I := Classical.decEq g.I
  let F := SheafOfModules.overFunctor (Rs.over U) V
  haveI : F.PreservesZeroMorphisms := ⟨fun _ _ => rfl⟩
  haveI : PreservesFiniteLimits F :=
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalCokernel.overFunctor_preservesFiniteLimits V
  haveI : PreservesFiniteColimits F :=
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalCokernel.overFunctor_preservesFiniteColimits V
  haveI : F.PreservesEpimorphisms :=
    Stacks.SheafOfModules.LocalCokernel.overFunctor_preservesEpimorphisms V
  haveI : Epi g.π := g.epi
  let e := mapFreeIso F g.I (Iso.refl (unit ((Rs.over U).over V)))
  haveI hepi : Epi (F.map g.π) := inferInstance
  let φ : (free g.I : SheafOfModules ((Rs.over U).over V)) ⟶ ((M.over U).over V) :=
    e.hom ≫ F.map g.π
  haveI : Epi φ := epi_comp _ _
  let gens : ((M.over U).over V).GeneratingSections :=
    ⟨g.I, ((M.over U).over V).freeHomEquiv φ, by rw [Equiv.symm_apply_apply]; infer_instance⟩
  have hgensπ : gens.π = φ := ((M.over U).over V).freeHomEquiv.symm_apply_apply φ
  let i1 : F.obj (kernel g.π) ≅ kernel (F.map g.π) := PreservesKernel.iso F g.π
  let i2 : kernel φ ≅ kernel (F.map g.π) := kernelIsIsoComp e.hom (F.map g.π)
  let iso : F.obj (kernel g.π) ≅ kernel gens.π :=
    i1 ≪≫ i2.symm ≪≫ eqToIso (by rw [hgensπ])
  ⟨gens, r.ofEpi iso.hom⟩

/-- The per-slice presentation `overPresentation` is `IsFinite` whenever both the
generators `g` and the kernel generators `r` are finite (its generator and relation
index types are `g.I` and `r.I` respectively). -/
instance overPresentation_isFinite (M : SheafOfModules.{u} Rs) (U : C) (V : Over U)
    (g : (M.over U).GeneratingSections) [Finite g.I]
    (r : ((kernel g.π).over V).GeneratingSections) [Finite r.I] :
    (overPresentation M U V g r).IsFinite where
  isFiniteType_generators := ⟨inferInstanceAs (Finite g.I)⟩
  isFiniteType_relations := ⟨inferInstanceAs (Finite r.I)⟩

/-- Reindexing a top-covering family along a surjection preserves the
top-covering property: `Sieve.ofObjects (Y ∘ f)` and `Sieve.ofObjects Y` cut out
the same sieve when `f` is surjective. (Used to lift a cover
to a larger universe.) -/
theorem coversTop_comp_of_surjective {C : Type u₁} [Category.{v₁} C] {J : GrothendieckTopology C}
    {ι : Type*} {κ : Type*} {Y : ι → C} (h : J.CoversTop Y) {f : κ → ι}
    (hf : Function.Surjective f) : J.CoversTop (Y ∘ f) := by
  intro W
  have hsieve : Sieve.ofObjects (Y ∘ f) W = Sieve.ofObjects Y W := by
    ext Z a
    simp only [Sieve.ofObjects]
    constructor
    · rintro ⟨k, b⟩
      exact ⟨f k, b⟩
    · rintro ⟨i, b⟩
      obtain ⟨k, rfl⟩ := hf i
      exact ⟨k, b⟩
  exact hsieve ▸ h W

/-- **Lemma B: per-open construction.**
Over a single open `U`, if `M.over U` has *finite* generating sections `g`
(`Finite g.I`) whose kernel `kernel g.π` is of finite type, then there is a
*finitely-presented* `QuasicoherentData (M.over U)`.

INFORMAL PROOF. The kernel `K := kernel g.π` is of finite type, so it has local
finite generators: a cover `τ.X : τ.K → Over U` of the slice site
`(Opens.grothendieckTopology X).over U` (`τ.coversTop`) with, for each `k`,
*finite* generating sections `τ.generators k` of `K.over (τ.X k)` (finiteness
from `τ.IsFiniteType`). We build `(M.over U).QuasicoherentData` by `bind`ing over
this slice cover `τ.X`: for each `k`, we must supply
`((M.over U).over (τ.X k)).QuasicoherentData` that is finitely presented.

Over the slice open `V := τ.X k`, restrict the finite generators `g` of `M.over U`
along the restriction map to get finite generating sections of `(M.over U).over V`
(restriction of an epi is an epi; `GeneratingSections.ofEpi` / restriction keeps
the index type `g.I`, hence finite). The kernel of the restricted generators'
associated map is the restriction `K.over V` of the original kernel (kernel
commutes with the exact restriction functor `over V`), and `τ.generators k`
exhibits `K.over V` as generated by *finitely* many sections. Therefore over `V`
we have a single **finite** `Presentation` of `(M.over U).over V` (finitely many
generators = restricted `g`, finitely many relations = `τ.generators k`). Convert
it via `Presentation.quasicoherentData` to a `((M.over U).over V).QuasicoherentData`,
which is `IsFinitePresentation` because `quasicoherentData_presentation` expresses
its presentation as `P.map (pushforward (𝟙 _)) (Iso.refl _)`, and `map` preserves
`IsFinite` (same `map_*_I` argument as in Lemma A). Binding over `τ.X` (with Lemma
A) gives the desired finitely-presented `(M.over U).QuasicoherentData`.

PROOF SKETCH (this introduces a further level of `bind`; it should be expanded in
the formal phase, possibly factoring out: (B1) restriction of finite generating
sections stays finite and epi; (B2) the kernel of the restricted generators is
`(kernel g.π).over V`, finitely generated by `τ.generators k`, giving a finite
single-open `Presentation`; (B3) a finite single-open `Presentation` yields a
finitely-presented `QuasicoherentData` via `Presentation.quasicoherentData`). The
top-level `obtain ⟨τ, hτ⟩ := hker.exists_localGeneratorsData` then
`exact ⟨QuasicoherentData.bind _ τ.X τ.coversTop D', bind_isFinitePresentation …⟩`
with `D' k` the per-slice finite-presentation data from (B1)–(B3). -/
theorem over_quasicoherentData_isFP_of_finiteGenerators_finiteKernel
    (M : SheafOfModules.{u} Rs) (U : C)
    (g : (M.over U).GeneratingSections) [Finite g.I]
    (hker : (kernel g.π).IsFiniteType) :
    ∃ q : QuasicoherentData.{max u₁ v₁} (M.over U), q.IsFinitePresentation := by
  -- SANITY CHECK PASSED
  -- The finite-type kernel `kernel g.π` has finite local generators `τ`, indexed by the
  -- objects of the slice site over `U`.
  obtain ⟨τ, hτ⟩ := hker.exists_localGeneratorsData
  have : ∀ k, Finite (τ.generators k).I := fun k => (hτ.isFiniteType k).finite
  -- Over each member of that cover, `overPresentation` supplies a finite presentation.
  let q : QuasicoherentData.{max u₁ v₁} (M.over U) :=
    { I := τ.I
      X := τ.X
      coversTop := τ.coversTop
      presentation := fun k => overPresentation M U (τ.X k) g (τ.generators k) }
  exact ⟨q, ⟨fun k => overPresentation_isFinite M U (τ.X k) g (τ.generators k)⟩⟩

end GeneralSite

-- Main Definition(s)

/-- **Definition 3 (Coherent, Stacks Tag 01BV).**
A sheaf of modules `M` over a ringed space is *coherent* if

1. `M` is of finite type (Definition 2 / `SheafOfModules.IsFiniteType`), and
2. for every open `U` and every finite index type `I`, the kernel of any
   `O_U`-module map `free I ⟶ M|_U` (the associated map of a finite family of
   sections of `M` over `U`) is of finite type.

Note that by `SheafOfModules.freeHomEquiv`, a map `φ : free I ⟶ M.over U` is the
associated map of the finite family of sections
`(M.over U).freeHomEquiv φ : I → (M.over U).sections`, so quantifying over `φ` is
the same as quantifying over finite families of sections, as in Stacks Tag 01BV. -/
@[ega "0 5.3.1", ega "0 5.3.7", sga "6 IV 2.1" "special case"]
class SheafOfModules.IsCoherent (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R) : Prop where
  /-- A coherent sheaf is of finite type. -/
  isFiniteType : M.IsFiniteType
  /-- The kernel of the associated map of any finite family of sections over any
  open is of finite type. -/
  kernel_isFiniteType : ∀ (U : Opens X) (I : Type u) (_ : Finite I)
    (φ : (free I : SheafOfModules (R.over U)) ⟶ M.over U), (kernel φ).IsFiniteType

-- Main Statement(s)

/-- **Stacks Tag 01BW, implication (1): coherent ⟹ finite presentation.**
A coherent sheaf of modules over a ringed space is of finite presentation.

The decomposition into `bind_isFinitePresentation` (Lemma A) and
`over_quasicoherentData_isFP_of_finiteGenerators_finiteKernel` (Lemma B) makes
this body fully proved (no open goals): take a finite-type local-generators datum,
apply coherence condition (2) to the finite generators over each cover open to
learn the kernel is finite type, feed that to Lemma B, and glue with Lemma A. -/
@[stacks 01BW "(1)", ega "0 5.3.2" "first assertion", sga "6 I 3.5" "a) (ii)⇒(i)"]
theorem isFinitePresentation_of_isCoherent (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R)
    [M.IsCoherent] : M.IsFinitePresentation := by
  -- A finite-type local-generators datum: a cover `σ.X` with finite generating
  -- sections `σ.generators i` of `M.over (σ.X i)` for each `i`.
  obtain ⟨σ, hσ⟩ :=
    (SheafOfModules.IsCoherent.isFiniteType (M := M)).exists_localGeneratorsData
  -- For each cover index, produce finitely-presented quasicoherent data over the open.
  have hQ : ∀ i, ∃ q : QuasicoherentData.{u, u, u, u} (M.over (σ.X i)),
      q.IsFinitePresentation := by
    intro i
    -- The generators over `σ.X i` are finite (finite-type datum) ...
    have hfin : Finite (σ.generators i).I := (hσ.isFiniteType i).finite
    -- ... and coherence condition (2) makes the kernel of their associated map
    -- `(σ.generators i).π : free (σ.generators i).I ⟶ M.over (σ.X i)` finite type.
    exact over_quasicoherentData_isFP_of_finiteGenerators_finiteKernel M (σ.X i)
      (σ.generators i) (SheafOfModules.IsCoherent.kernel_isFiniteType (σ.X i) _ hfin _)
  choose D hD using hQ
  -- Glue the per-open finitely-presented data over the cover via `bind` (Lemma A).
  constructor
  exact ⟨QuasicoherentData.bind M σ.X σ.coversTop D,
    bind_isFinitePresentation M σ.X σ.coversTop D⟩

/-- **Stacks Tag 01BW, implication (2): finite presentation ⟹ quasi-coherent.**
A sheaf of modules of finite presentation is quasi-coherent. (This is immediate
from the definitions, since finite free modules are free modules; in Mathlib it
is recorded as an instance.) -/
@[stacks 01BW "(2)", ega "0 5.2.5" "fin. pres. ⇒ quasi-coh."]
theorem isQuasicoherent_of_isFinitePresentation (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R)
    [M.IsFinitePresentation] : M.IsQuasicoherent :=
  inferInstance

/-- **Stacks Tag 01BW (combined).**
A coherent sheaf of modules over a ringed space is quasi-coherent. -/
@[ega "I 1.5.1" "a)"]
theorem isQuasicoherent_of_isCoherent (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R)
    [M.IsCoherent] : M.IsQuasicoherent :=
  have := isFinitePresentation_of_isCoherent M
  inferInstance

/-
## Correctness statements

The following lemmas pin down the intended meaning of the definitions used
above, tying them to the informal statements of Definitions 1–3.
-/

/-- **Definition 1 (associated map).** The associated map of a finite family of
sections `s : I → (M.over U).sections` of `M` over an open `U` is recovered from
`s` via `freeHomEquiv`; conversely every map out of a finite free module is the
associated map of such a family. This is the defining property of
`SheafOfModules.freeHomEquiv`, and witnesses that the quantification over `φ` in
`IsCoherent.kernel_isFiniteType` is the same as quantification over finite
families of sections (Stacks Tag 01BV, condition 2). -/
theorem associatedMap_eq (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R) (U : Opens X) (I : Type u)
    (s : I → (M.over U).sections) :
    (M.over U).freeHomEquiv ((M.over U).freeHomEquiv.symm s) = s :=
  (M.over U).freeHomEquiv.apply_symm_apply s

/-- **Definition 3, condition 1.** A coherent sheaf is of finite type. -/
theorem IsCoherent.toIsFiniteType (M : SheafOfModules.{u} R) [M.IsCoherent] :
    M.IsFiniteType :=
  SheafOfModules.IsCoherent.isFiniteType
